  "response": "약혼자분이 \"당신 생각을 듣고 싶다\"고 했을 때 머릿속이 하얘졌다는 경험, 그 순간이 꽤 당혹스러우셨을 것 같아요. 그리고 그 이후로 계속 되짚고 계신 것도요.\n\n\"이상하잖아요\"라고 하셨는데… 서른한 해 동안 \"어머니한테 여쭤보고\"가 자연스럽게 몸에 배어 있었다면, 자기 생각을 꺼내는 데 익숙하지 않은 건 이상한 게 아니라 충분히 이해가 되는 면이 있어요. 없는 게 아니라, 연습할 기회가 적었던 건 아닐까 하는 생각이 드는데… 어떻게 느껴지세요?",
  "reasoning": "The person has come to counseling not by their own choice but because their fiancé gave an ultimatum. They've shared a significant realization: they keep deferring to their mother's opinions when making decisions about wedding preparations, and when their fiancé asked them to express their own thoughts, they experienced a complete blank - no ideas, no preferences came up.\n\nThey're questioning whether something is wrong with them - \"Isn't it strange? Usually people have at least one thing they like or want.\"\n\nKey things to notice:\n1. They came reluctantly - they don't fully own this process yet\n2. They're experiencing a genuine moment of confusion/distress about not knowing their own preferences\n3. They're self-questioning in a way that carries shame (\"이상하잖아요\")\n4. The pattern of deferring to mother is clear and they've identified it themselves\n5. They're not asking for advice - they're sharing a discovery and expressing confusion\n\nWhat would be helpful here?\n- Validate that this is a real and painful experience, not dismiss it\n- Not rush to \"well you probably do have preferences, they're just buried\" - that's premature and could feel dismissive\n- Acknowledge the courage it took to notice this pattern\n- The shame around \"not knowing what I want\" is worth addressing - it's not strange, it can develop when someone has learned to defer\n- I should not be overly reassuring or minimize. The person is genuinely disturbed by this.\n- I could gently reflect back what they've noticed and perhaps normalize that this can happen when someone has learned from an early age that their own preferences aren't prioritized or safe to express - but I need to be careful not to speculate about their family dynamics without evidence.\n\nI think the most helpful response would:\n1. Acknowledge the discomfort of being in a situation they didn't choose\n2. Honor what they've noticed - that this blank moment was significant and painful\n3. Gently push back on the self-judgment (\"이상하잖아요\") without being dismissive - perhaps by noting that if someone has consistently learned to defer, the muscle of knowing one's own preferences may not have had much practice, which is different from \"not having any\"\n4. Maybe a gentle question that helps them explore further\n\nLet me draft:\n\nSomething like: \"여기 오신 게 본인이 원해서가 아니었다는 것, 그리고 그 상황에서 머릿속이 하얘졌다는 경험 자체가 꽤 당황스러우셨을 것 같아요.\"\n\nThen address the self-judgment: \"'서른한 살인데 아무것도 모른다'는 말에서 스스로를 꽤 엄하게 보고 계신 것 같은데… 한 번 여쭤봐도 될까요? 어머니한테 여쭤보는 게 '당연한 순서'처럼 느껴지는 순간이 있었을 것 같은데, 그건 언제부터 그랬을까요?\"\n\nHmm, I want to be careful. The last question might be too much too soon - they're still in the stage of processing the shock of the realization. Maybe I should stay closer to their experience first.\n\nLet me try again:\n\n\"약혼자분이 '당신 생각을 듣고 싶다'고 했을 때 머릿속이 하얘졌다는 경험, 그 순간이 꽤 당혹스러우셨을 것 같아요. 그리고 그 이후로 '나한테 뭘 좋아하는 게 정말 없나' 하고 계속 생각하셨다는 것도요.\n\n'이상하잖아요'라고 하셨는데… 서른한 해 동안 '어머니한테 여쭤보고'가 자연스럽게 몸에 배어 있었다면, 자기 생각을 꺼내는 데 익숙하지 않은 건 이상한 게 아니라 충분히 이해가 되는 면이 있어요. 없는 게 아니라, 연습할 기회가 적었던 건 아닐까 하는 생각이 드는데… 어떻게 느껴지세요?\"\n\nI think this is good. It:\n- Validates the experience\n- Gently reframes the self-judgment without being dismissive\n- Offers a possible reframe (not \"you don't have preferences\" but \"you haven't had much practice\") as a hypothesis, not a fact\n- Ends with an invitation to reflect, not a demand\n\nThis feels natural and not too long. Let me finalize.\n",
